::ENG3_U01_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision best replaces vague wording with academic vocabulary? Vague sentence: The speaker says good things to make people agree. {
=A. The speaker uses positive diction to persuade the audience.#Correct. This revision names the language choice, purpose, and audience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The speaker says nice stuff so people like it.#This is still vague because nice stuff and people like it do not identify the technique or purpose.
~C. The speaker talks in a way that is good.#This does not provide precise academic vocabulary. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The speaker has words that help everything.#This is too general and does not explain the speaker's strategy. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best means trustworthy and believable? {
~A. Precise#Precise means exact and specific, not necessarily trustworthy. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Credible#Correct. Credible means trustworthy and believable. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Purpose#Purpose means the reason for communicating. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Audience#Audience means the people a writer or speaker is trying to reach. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses audience correctly? {
~A. The audience is the reason the speaker communicates.#This defines purpose, not audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The audience is the exact information used for support.#This defines evidence, not audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The audience is the group the speaker wants to reach and influence.#Correct. Audience refers to the people receiving the message. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The audience is the device used to create emphasis.#This describes a technique, not the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision would work best in a short spoken explanation? {
~A. The thing is about stuff that matters to people.#This is too vague for an English III speaking note. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The author does good writing and makes a point.#This is general and does not use precise academic vocabulary. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The text has words and people respond.#This does not explain the vocabulary relationship clearly. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The author uses evidence to support a purpose that the audience can understand.#Correct. The sentence is clear enough to say aloud and uses academic vocabulary accurately. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U01_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which academic word best fits this sentence? The repeated phrase is a rhetorical _____ because it helps persuade the audience. {
~A. claim#A claim is a position or argument, not the technique being used. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. device#Correct. A rhetorical device is a technique used to affect an audience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. source#A source provides information; it is not the repeated phrase itself. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. definition#A definition explains meaning; it is not the technique. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
